{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310646,"nonce":"02ZqYNSTjFwUs6324YgKTGuTZEk=","title":"OPEN — closures made in a `for` loop and stored all capture the loop variable's FINAL value; Haxe gives each iteration its own binding","message":"**REPRO** (`/tmp/probe/D1.hx`):\n```haxe\nvar arr:Array\u003cVoid-\u003eInt\u003e = [];\nfor (i in 0...2) arr.push(function() return i);\narr[0]();   // 2   -- want 0\narr[1]();   // 2   -- want 1\n```\n`for (i in 0...2) { var g = function() return i; g(); }` prints 0 then 1\nCORRECTLY — calling in the same iteration hides it. Only a closure that\nOUTLIVES its iteration shows the shared slot.\n\nHaxe gives each iteration a fresh binding, so the captures are 0 and 1. Here\nevery closure captures the one mutable slot, which holds 2 after the loop.\n\n**MEASURED 2026-09-11 — THE COUNTER ITSELF IS WHAT GETS CAPTURED.** MIR for\nthe repro:\n\n    $13 = const 8u64\n    $14 = call fn0($13)      ; the loop counter, heap-allocated\n    store $14, $12           ; = 0\n    ...\n    $22 = load i32 $14       ; loop condition reads it\n    $30 = make_closure fn4, [$14]   ; and the closure captures THE COUNTER\n\nSo every closure shares the counter cell, which holds the final value once the\nloop ends. A per-iteration binding has to COPY the loaded value into a fresh\ncell inside the body and have the closure capture that.\n\n**FIXED 2026-09-11 in 32018b5d, TWO PARTS:**\n1. `ast_lowering/expr/loops.rs` — the range `for` desugared to\n   `var i = start; while (i \u003c end) { body; i++ }`, driving the loop with `i`\n   ITSELF. Now a separate `i__counter` drives it and `var i = \u003ccounter\u003e;`\n   (Immutable) heads the body, so each iteration binds its own.\n2. `mir/expr/lambda.rs` + `mir/helpers/patterns.rs` — added\n   `box_capture_binding_fresh`, used on the DECLARATION path. The existing\n   `box_capture_binding` reuses a symbol's cell, which is right for an\n   assignment but wrong for a declaration: reuse is what let successive\n   iterations share a slot even once `i` was declared inside the body.\n\n**HOW THE ROUTE WAS FOUND** (after the first attempt failed): a probe at the\nlambda capture loop printed\n`sym=i mode=ByMutableRef symbol_map=Some(IrId(22)) cell=Some(IrId(14))` —\n`ByMutableRef` takes `capture_cells` (the shared counter) in preference to\n`symbol_map` (the correctly-loaded per-iteration value). INSTRUMENT THE\nCAPTURE LOOP FIRST for anything in this area; the mode and the two candidate\nregisters are all visible in one line.\n\n**FIRST ATTEMPT REVERTED (2026-09-11) — WRONG FILE.** Clearing `capture_cells`\nin `mir/stmt/for_in.rs` was inert because a RANGE `for` never reaches\nfor_in.rs at all: it is desugared to a while loop in ast_lowering. Check which\nlowering actually runs before editing one. Clearing `capture_cells` for the loop\nsymbol at the body binding in `lower_for_in_range`\n(`compiler/src/ir/mir/stmt/for_in.rs`, \"Body block: reload the counter and\nbind it to the pattern variable\") was INERT — the MIR is unchanged, still\n`make_closure [$14]`. So the capture does NOT go through\n`box_capture_binding` (mir/expr/lambda.rs) in the body; it resolves the symbol\nto the counter pointer by another route. **Find that route first** — put a\nprobe in `box_capture_binding` and in the lambda capture loop\n(lambda.rs:129) and confirm whether either runs for a loop variable, rather\nthan assuming the cell cache is the mechanism.\n\n**FOUND ALONGSIDE (both FIXED):** `map` losing its element type\n(97bd7a69) and the crash it caused in `join`. The loop-capture bug survived\nthose fixes — `viamap=2,2` in the same probe.\n\nNo corpus row is known to depend on this; it was found by probing, like the\nrest of the [[feedback_probe_ordinary_semantics_finds_real_bugs]] family.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_loop_closures_share_one_slot` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}